Article 4 In a case of dispute over property division arising from a cohabitation relationship where neither party has a spouse, if there is an agreement on the property acquired during cohabitation, the agreement shall prevail; if there is no agreement and negotiation fails, the People's Court shall deal with the matter separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) The wages, bonuses, remuneration for personal services, proceeds of intellectual property rights acquired by each party, property inherited or donated to each party, and the proceeds from individual production, operation, or investment shall belong to each party respectively;
(2) Property purchased with joint contributions or proceeds from joint production, operation, or investment, and other property that cannot be distinguished, shall be divided based on the proportion of each party's contribution, taking into comprehensive consideration the circumstances of their common life, whether they have common children, the extent of contribution to the property, and other factors.

Article 14 In divorce proceedings, if both parents request to directly care for a minor child who has reached the age of two, and one party falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall, in accordance with the principle of the best interests of the minor child, give priority to the other party to directly care for the child:
(1) Committing domestic violence or abusing or abandoning family members;
(2) Having bad habits such as gambling or drug abuse;
(3) Bigamy, cohabiting with another person, or other serious violations of the duty of fidelity;
(4) Forcibly taking away or hiding the minor child, and the other party does not fall under the circumstances of subparagraph (1) or (2) of this paragraph or other serious infringement of the lawful rights and interests of the minor child;
(5) Other circumstances detrimental to the physical and mental health of the minor child.
